# Kotlin/Android coding style

Narrows `core-common-rules`' style rules to the Kotlin coding conventions and Android idiom.

## Naming and structure

- A `@Composable` that emits UI is named as a noun (`UserProfileCard`); one that
  returns a value (`rememberX`) is named as a normal function.
- One primary class/object per file named after it, small private helpers
  colocated; no shared `Utils.kt`.

## Data classes vs regular classes

- `data class` only for a type whose identity is its held values (DTO, UI state,
  value object); never for an `Activity`, repository or singleton manager.
- Prefer `copy()` with named parameters over a hand-built constructor call.
- A `data class` holding UI state defaults every field so `UiState()` alone is valid.

## Null-safety idiom

- Prefer `?.` or `?:` over `!!`; reserve `!!` for a value a prior check has
  proven non-null, never to get past a nullability warning.
- Prefer `requireNotNull(x) { "why x must be non-null here" }` over `x!!` at a
  boundary (constructor, public function entry).
- Use `value?.let { ... }` when the block needs the unwrapped value once; do not
  chain several `?.let` where one `if (value != null)` smart-cast reads better.
- Model "value or absent" with a nullable or sealed result type, not a sentinel
  (`-1`, `""`).

## Sealed classes/interfaces for state modeling

- Model UI or domain state as a `sealed interface`/`sealed class`
  (`Loading`, `Success(data)`, `Error(throwable)`), not one class with nullable
  fields and an `isLoading` boolean.
- `sealed interface` unless the variants share state or behavior; then `sealed class`.
- Keep a sealed hierarchy's variants in the same file as the parent.

## Extension functions

- Use an extension for a type this module does not own, or to keep a small helper
  out of a growing class; not in place of a method on a type that is yours to edit.
- Keep the receiver narrow and the name unambiguous (`String.toDisplayDate()`,
  not `Any.format()`).
- No extensions on core types (`String`, `Int`, `List`) for one-off,
  feature-specific logic; put them on a feature-scoped type.
